<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Imamate of Oman</h1><p class="st-meta">1913 CE – 1959 CE · capital Nizwa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The 20th-century Ibadi imamate of interior Oman, autonomous from the sultan of Muscat under the Treaty of Seeb until the Jebel Akhdar War.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Interior tribes resented the coastal <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-muscat-and-oman">Sultanate of Muscat and Oman</a>, its British ties and its curbs on the arms trade. In 1913 Ibadi scholars and tribal leaders elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salim-ibn-rashid-al-kharusi">Salim ibn Rashid al-Kharusi</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanuf">Tanuf</a>; his forces took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizwa">Nizwa</a> and threatened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a>, which was saved by British Indian troops.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">imamate</a> revived the old Ibadi ideal of an elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> ruling by Islamic law, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizwa">Nizwa</a> as capital and tribal leaders such as Isa ibn <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/salihids">Salih</a> al-Harthi as its strongmen. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-seeb">Treaty of Seeb</a> of 1920 gave the interior wide autonomy. Under Imam <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-khalili">Muhammad al-Khalili</a>, from 1920 to 1954, it remained isolated and conservative, with little trade or modern administration.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-khalili">al-Khalili</a>'s death, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghalib-alhinai">Ghalib ibn Ali</a> sought outside recognition as oil exploration began. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/said-bin-taimur">Said bin Taimur's</a> forces, backed by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a>, occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizwa">Nizwa</a> in 1955, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebel-akhdar-war">Jebel Akhdar War</a> ended in 1959 when British troops stormed the mountain. The leaders went into exile.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/imamate-of-oman.jpg" alt="Imamate of Oman" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: BlinxTheKitty · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Imamate_of_Oman_(1954%E2%80%931959).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1913 CE – 1959 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nizwa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Salim ibn Rashid al-Kharusi, Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Khalili, Ghalib ibn Ali al-Hinai</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ibadi Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/oman">Oman</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Imamate of Oman (1913-1959), Omani Imamate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1936&amp;focus=imamate-of-oman" aria-label="Imamate of Oman on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6217 1331 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Imamate of Oman lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6537 1587L6562 1563"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6562 1563h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6537 1587h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6537 1587h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Nizwa. <a href="/atlas?year=1936&amp;focus=imamate-of-oman">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
